---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Project | NutriBean+">
  <div class="page-container">
    <section class="intro-card-white">
      <h1>Introduction</h1>
      <p>
        NutriBean+ is a project based on the philosophy of "The Farm to Fork Strategy", a set of policies which aim to make Europe the first sustainable continent by 2050 by reducing the use of pesticides as well as the waste of water and by promoting a healthy and balanced diet, which should be accessible to everyone, and a model of circular economy. This is where legumes come into play, as they have many characteristics which are functional to the strategy's objectives. They do not require any nitrogen-based fertilizer and have a very low water footprint. We chose chickpeas and soybeans, which are the most widely grown legumes in Italy. These legumes represent the main source of plant-based proteins, and Italy is Europe's top producer, especially of soybean. These legumes are mainly used for animal feed and for the production of isolates, and protein supplements, thanks to their high nutritional levels, and so far only the grains have been harvested, whereas the pods are left in the fields.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="step-list">
      <article class="step-row">
        <div class="step-image">
          <img src="/projects-1.jpg" alt="Studenti in laboratorio durante le analisi" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <span class="step-number">01</span>
          <h2>Our project</h2>
          <p>
            The target of our project is to promote the farming and consumption of soy and chickpeas by carrying out a varietal characterization of the different ground powders, and by trying to find new sources for a healthy nutrition. We're also trying to encourage the use of soy pods as an ingredient in flours and supplements due to their extremely high antioxidant power and protein content.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="step-row reverse">
        <div class="step-image">
          <img src="/projects-2.jpg" alt="Campo sperimentale a Casale Monferrato" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <span class="step-number">02</span>
          <h2>Agricultural aspects</h2>
          <p>
            Prior to the chemical analysis, we focused on the agricultural aspect of the project, by obtaining a comprehensive screening of both soybeans and chickpeas. All the examined varieties came from an experimental varietal field and all the soybean by-products were obtained from a field in Casale Monferrato.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="step-row">
        <div class="step-image">
          <img src="/projects-3.jpeg" alt="Provette con campioni per parametri fisici" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <span class="step-number">03</span>
          <h2>Physical parameters</h2>
          <p>
            We also focused on defining the physical parameters such as the water and oil absorption capacities. The former is affected by the different types of fibers, and the latter by the presence of apolar amino acid chains, which are essential data in the food industry as they determine the texture and flavour of the different products.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="step-row reverse">
        <div class="step-image">
          <img src="/projects-4.jpg" alt="Analisi nutraceutiche e nutrizionali in laboratorio" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <span class="step-number">04</span>
          <h2>Nutraceutical and Nutritional content</h2>
          <p>
            We have also evaluated the nutraceutical and nutritional parameters starting with the total polyphenols and the antioxidant power which show that Soybean pods have an extraordinary level of both, almost double that of the other samples, making them suitable for the use in supplements, thanks to their ability to counteract the chain propagation of free radicals. We also determined the lipids, fibers, protein and sugar content. We found out that soy pods have a very low percentage of lipids (&lt;1% w/w) and a high amount of proteins (27.6% w/w) which is even higher than that of chickpeas.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="step-row">
        <div class="step-image">
          <img src="/projects-5.jpeg" alt="Campioni di farine e baccelli" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <span class="step-number">05</span>
          <h2>Cost estimation</h2>
          <p>
            Lastly, we have examined the crops' total production cost and we found out that the values for each of our samples differs little from each other, except for the fertilization, irrigation, and weeding costs. In fact, soybeans require more soil fertilization prior to sowing and a greater use of phytosanitary products compared to chickpeas. The cost of irrigation for the latter is negligible, but during droughts the production decreases significantly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <div class="bottom-cta">
      <a href="/analysis" class="btn-discover">FINAL RESULTS &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
